[{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"CurrentModule = TransferFunctions","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"TransferFunction","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.TransferFunction","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.TransferFunction","text":"An N-dimensional transfer function\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"MeasuredPSF\nMeasuredOTF","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.MeasuredPSF","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.MeasuredPSF","text":"MeasuredPSF holds an array of measured data with information about the dimensions of the measurement. This allows you to make conversions to other representations of a transfer function such as an OTF and to use interpolations to sample the PSF at arbitrary locations not included in the initial measurement (in general to use the measurements  similarly to how one could use a model). This can be useful for using the same PSF measurement for an acquisition with  a different pixelsize or in super resolution applications.\n\nA measurement can be done for example using an image of subresolution microspheres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.MeasuredOTF","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.MeasuredOTF","text":"MeasuredOTF holds an array of measured data with information about the dimensions of the measurement. This allows you to make conversions to other representations of a transfer function such as a PSF and to use interpolations to sample the OTF at arbitrary locations not included in the initial measurement (in general to use the measurements  similarly to how one could use a model). This can be useful for using the same OTF measurement for an acquisition with  a different pixelsize or in super resolution applications.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"SampledTransferFunction","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.SampledTransferFunction","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.SampledTransferFunction","text":"TODO\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#Functions","page":"API","title":"Functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"psf\nipsf\napsf\notf\npupil\nptf\nmtf","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.psf","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.psf","text":"Intensity point spread function i.e. the intensity ratio and phase shift of the sample intensity density\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.ipsf","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.ipsf","text":"Intensity point spread function\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.apsf","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.apsf","text":"Amplitude point spread function\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.otf","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.otf","text":"optical transfer function\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.pupil","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.pupil","text":"pupil function\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.ptf","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.ptf","text":"phase transfer function\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.mtf","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.mtf","text":"modulation transfer function\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#Utility-Functions-and-Traits","page":"API","title":"Utility Functions and Traits","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"roundupcenter\nexactcenter","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.roundupcenter","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.roundupcenter","text":"roundupcenter(arr::AbstractArray)\n\nCalculate center index of arr rounded-up (i.e. fft center)\n\nExamples\n\njulia> roundupcenter(ones(15,15))\n(8, 8)\n\njulia> roundupcenter(ones(16,16))\n(9, 9)\n\njulia> roundupcenter(ones(15,15,15))\n(8, 8, 8)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.exactcenter","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.exactcenter","text":" exactcenter(a::AbstractArray)\n\nCalculate the exact center index of a\n\nExamples\n\njulia> exactcenter(ones(15,15))\n(8.0, 8.0)\n\njulia> exactcenter(ones(16,16))\n(8.5, 8.5)\n\njulia> exactcenter(ones(15,15,15))\n(8.0, 8.0, 8.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"RadiallySymmetric","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.RadiallySymmetric","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.RadiallySymmetric","text":"If the pupil function of the system is symmetric, the OTF as well as the PSF are radially symmteric which can be used to optimize the calculations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#Apodization","page":"API","title":"Apodization","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"apodize\ntaperedges","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.apodize","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.apodize","text":"apodize(apo::Apodization, A::AbstractArray{<:Number,N}, cutoff::Real, width::Real) where {N}\n\nApply apodization to the input array A.\n\nArguments\n\napo::Apodization: The type of apodization to apply.\nA::AbstractArray{<:Number,N}: The input array to be apodized.\ncutoff::Real: The cutoff frequency for the apodization.\nwidth::Real: The width of the transition region for the apodization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.taperedges","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.taperedges","text":"taperedges([apo], A, width::Int, [border=\"replicate\"]; dims=1:N)\ntaperedges([apo], A, (w1,...,wM), [border=\"replicate\"]; dims =1:M)\ntaperedges([apo], A, ((w1_start,...,wM_start), (w1_end,...,wM_end)), [border=\"replicate\"]; dims=1:M)\n\nTaper a given width of the array A's edges using apo::Apodization\n\napo: Apodization function to use for the tapering. Default is Cosine.\nborder=\"replicate\" : Similarly as with imfilter, the array A may be extended to avoid loss of the data at the edges\nwidth::NTuple{M,Int} or Int or Tuple{NTuple{M,Int},NTuple{M,Int}}: The width of tapering at the edges.\ndims::NTuple{M,Int}: The dimensions along which to taper the edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"Apodization Functions","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"Apodization","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Apodization","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Apodization","text":"Apodization\n\nAbstract type for apodization functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"Blackman\nExactBlackman\nConnes\nCosine\nGaussian\nHamming\nWelch\nBlackmanNuttall\nPowerCosine\nTriangular\nNuttall\nSineSum\nBlackmanHarris\nFlatTop\nHann","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Blackman","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Blackman","text":"Blackman{α::Real} == SineSum{3, ((1 - α) / 2, 1 / 2, α / 2) } <: Apodization\n\nDefault is α = 0.16.\n\nSee also ExactBlackman\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.ExactBlackman","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.ExactBlackman","text":"ExactBlackman == Blackman{683 // 4652}\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Connes","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Connes","text":"Connes(a::Real) <: Apodization\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Cosine","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Cosine","text":"Cosine == PowerCosine{1} <: Apodization\n\nzero-phase function: w₀(r) = cos(πr2)\ninstrument function: I(k) = 4cos(2k)(π(1 - 16k²))\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Gaussian","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Gaussian","text":"Gaussian(σ::Real) <: Apodization\n\nGaussian apodization function with a given standard deviation σ.\n\nzero-phase function: w₀(r) = e^-r²2σ²\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Hamming","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Hamming","text":"Hamming == SineSum{2, (25//46, 21//46) } <: Apodization\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Welch","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Welch","text":"Welch() <: Apodization\n\nzero-phase function: w₀(r) = 1 - r²\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.BlackmanNuttall","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.BlackmanNuttall","text":"BlackmanNuttall == SineSum{4, (0.3635819, 0.4891775, 0.1365995, 0.0106411) } <: Apodization\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.PowerCosine","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.PowerCosine","text":"PowerCosine{α<:Real} <: Apodization\n\nzero-phase function: w₀(r) = cos(πr2)^α\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Triangular","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Triangular","text":"Triangular <: Apodization\n\nFormulas:\n\nzero-phase function: w₀(r) = 1-r\ninstrument function: I(k) = mathopsinc²(π k)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Nuttall","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Nuttall","text":"Nuttall == SineSum{4, (0.355768, 0.487396, 0.144232, 0.012604) } <: Apodization\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.SineSum","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.SineSum","text":"SineSum{N::UInt, Cs::NTuple{N, Real}} <: Apodization\n\nzero-phase function: w₀(r) = ᴺₖ₀ (-1)ᵏ Csk cos(πk(r + 1))\n\nSpecific instances with coefficients Hamming, Nuttall, BlackmanNuttall, BlackmanHarris, FlatTop, Blackman and ExactBlackman\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.BlackmanHarris","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.BlackmanHarris","text":"BlackmanHarris == SineSum{4, (0.35875, 0.48829, 0.14128, 0.01168) } <: Apodization\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.FlatTop","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.FlatTop","text":"FlatTop == SineSum{5, (0.21557895, 0.41663158, 0.277263158, 0.083578947, 0.006947365) } <: Apodization\n\nMATLAB varaint of the flat-top filter\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.Hann","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.Hann","text":"Hann == PowerCosine{2} <: Apodization\n\nFormulas:\n\nzero-phase function: w₀(r) = cos²(πr2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#Deprecations","page":"API","title":"Deprecations","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/","page":"API","title":"API","text":"padtosize","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/05_apireference/#TransferFunctions.padtosize","page":"API","title":"TransferFunctions.padtosize","text":"padtosize(a::AbstractArray{T,N}, size...; fourier=false, padvalue=0)\npadtosize(a::AbstractArray{T,N}, size; fourier=false, padvalue=0)\n\nPad a to a size\n\nPadding is done in a way which ensures that if a is symmetric and decreases to zero on the edges (!!) such that eltype(fft(a)) <: Real, then, if possible, output is symmetric under the DFT and elyptype(fft(a_padded)) <: Real). If a is in the Fourier domain, then fourier should de set to true.\n\nArguments:\n\nsize: size of the output array. If size isa Integer then size along all the dimensions is size\nfourier: a is in the Fourier domain (default: false).\n\nExamples:\n\njulia> padtosize(reshape(1:4, 2,2), 3, 3)\n3×3 Matrix{Int64}:\n 0  0  0\n 0  1  3\n 0  2  4\n\njulia> padtosize(reshape(1:4, 2,2), 4, 4)\n4×4 Matrix{Int64}:\n 0  0  0  0\n 0  1  3  0\n 0  2  4  0\n 0  0  0  0\n\njulia> padtosize(reshape(1:4, 2,2), 3, 3; fourier=true)\n3×3 Matrix{Int64}:\n 4  2  0\n 3  1  0\n 0  0  0\n\njulia> padtosize(reshape(1:4, 2,2), 4, 4; fourier=true)\n4×4 Matrix{Int64}:\n 1  0  0  3\n 0  0  0  0\n 0  0  0  0\n 2  0  0  4\n\njulia> ones(1,1) |> fft\n1×1 Matrix{ComplexF64}:\n 1.0 + 0.0im\n\njulia> padtosize(ones(1,1), 2,2) |> fft\n2×2 Matrix{ComplexF64}:\n  1.0+0.0im  -1.0+0.0im\n -1.0+0.0im   1.0+0.0im\n\njulia> ones(2,2) |> fft\n2×2 Matrix{ComplexF64}:\n 4.0+0.0im  0.0+0.0im\n 0.0+0.0im  0.0+0.0im\n\njulia> padtosize(ones(2,2), 3, 3) |> fft # can be made symmetric\n3×3 Matrix{ComplexF64}:\n  4.0+0.0im  -2.0+0.0im  -2.0+0.0im\n -2.0+0.0im   1.0+0.0im   1.0+0.0im\n -2.0+0.0im   1.0+0.0im   1.0+0.0im\n\njulia> padtosize(ones(2,2), 4, 4) |> fft # cannot be symmetric\n4×4 Matrix{ComplexF64}:\n  4.0+0.0im  -2.0-2.0im  0.0+0.0im  -2.0+2.0im\n -2.0-2.0im   0.0+2.0im  0.0+0.0im   2.0+0.0im\n  0.0+0.0im   0.0+0.0im  0.0+0.0im   0.0+0.0im\n -2.0+2.0im   2.0+0.0im  0.0+0.0im   0.0-2.0im\n\njulia> @assert all(-10^-5 .< imag(fft(padtosize(ones(2,2), 5, 5))) .< 10^-5) # .== 0 (numerical errors)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"Transfer Function Models","text":"CurrentModule = TransferFunctions","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/#OTF-Models","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"OTF Models","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"Transfer Function Models","text":"ModelOTF","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/#TransferFunctions.ModelOTF","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"TransferFunctions.ModelOTF","text":"Implementation\n\nTo create a new OTF model A <: ModelOTF, you need to implement the functions:\n\nThe transfer coefficient at a give frequency:\nattenuation(model::A, kx::Frequency, ky::Frequency) for a non-symmetric OTF, or\nattenuation(model::A, kᵣ::Frequency) for a symmetric OTF, where you also have to call @traitimpl   RadiallySymmetric{A}\n\nYou should consider implementing the following optional methods:\n\npreferred_type(::Type{<:ModelOTF}) method, which returns the natural type that is returned from calling the model.   It should be either <:Complex or <:Real.\n\nFor a symmetric OTF, you should consider implementing the optional methods:\n\ncutoff(model::A; a) which returns the largest frequency f::Frequency such that attenuation(model, f) >= a if    a > 0 and attenuation(model, f) > 0 if a == 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"Transfer Function Models","text":"IdealOTFwithCurvature","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/#TransferFunctions.IdealOTFwithCurvature","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"TransferFunctions.IdealOTFwithCurvature","text":"IdealCircularPupilOTF(ρ₀::Real)\n\nIdeal (aberration free) OTF of a diffraction limited imaging system with incoherent light with the cutoff-frequency ρ₀\n\nThe OTF is derived from the diffraction caused by the exit pupil of the system and disregards the effect of the entrance pupil... thus assumes no reshaping of the wavefronts in the optical system. The exit pupil, being located in the optical system just before the light reaches the image plane, has a greater effect on the optical system OTF.\n\nSee also IdealCircularPupilPSF (TODO)\n\nExamples\n\n- TODO\n\nExtended help\n\nThe OTF can be written in as a function of the cutoff frequency ρ₀ [1] \n\n    ℋ(ρ) = \n    begincases\n    (2π) left\n        arccos(ρ2ρ₀) - (ρ2ρ₀)sqrt1 - (ρ2ρ₀)²\n    right  text for  ρ  2ρ₀ \n        0  text otherwise\n    endcases\n\n\nThe cutoff frequency can be written in terms of the wavelength λ, distance between entrance pupil and the image plane f₂ and the circular pupil radius w as\n\n    rho_0 = w(λ f₂)\n\n[1]: Frequency Analysis of Optical Imaging Systems. In Introduction to Fourier optics; Roberts & Co: Englewood, Colo, 2005; pp. 127–172 ISBN 978-0-9747077-2-3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/#PSF-Models","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"PSF Models","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"Transfer Function Models","text":"BornWolf\nGibsonLanni","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/#TransferFunctions.BornWolf","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"TransferFunctions.BornWolf","text":"Born & Wolf model of the transfer function for a circular aperture.\n\nParameters\n\nλ::Unitful.Length: (emission) wavelength \nnᵢ::Number: index of refraction of the immersion medium\nNA::Number: numerical aperture\n\nExtended help\n\nThe Born & Wolf model is a scalar diffraction model derived for perfect systems. It assumes that the only aberration of the system is due to defocus. Modern microscope objectives are designed to provide optimal imaging conditions for sources located directly on the coverslip, in which case the Born & Wolf model is applicable (if the coverslip and immersion is used as designed). The model disregards spherical and higher order aberrations that are due to the source of illumination being shifted from the coverslip boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/03_model_tfs/#TransferFunctions.GibsonLanni","page":"Transfer Function Models","title":"TransferFunctions.GibsonLanni","text":"Gibson & Lanni model of the transfer function for a circular aperture.\n\nParameters\n\nExtended help\n\nThe Gibson & Lanni model is assumes that, disregarding defocus, all observed aberrations are generated by factors external to the objective (i.e. originating in the sample, coverslip and immersion medium combination). These aberrations can be characterized by the optical path difference between a ray in a perfect system (see BornWolf) and a ray under experimental conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/06_bibliography/#Bibliography","page":"Bibliography","title":"Bibliography","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/06_bibliography/","page":"Bibliography","title":"Bibliography","text":"M. R. Arnison and C. J. Sheppard. A 3D Vectorial Optical Transfer Function Suitable for Arbitrary Pupil Functions. Optics Communications 211, 53–63 (2002). Accessed on Jul 8, 2023.\n\n\n\nM. Born and E. Wolf. Principles of Optics: Electromagnetic Theory of Propagation, Interference and Diffraction of Light. 7th expanded ed Edition (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge ; New York, 1999).\n\n\n\nThe Airy Disk and Diffraction Limit, https://www.edmundoptics.com/knowledge-center/application-notes/imaging/limitations-on-resolution-and-contrast-the-airy-disk/. Accessed on Sep 21, 2023.\n\n\n\nResolution, https://www.edmundoptics.com/knowledge-center/application-notes/imaging/resolution/. Accessed on Sep 21, 2023.\n\n\n\nB. R. Frieden. Optical Transfer of the Three-Dimensional Object. JOSA 57, 56–66 (1967). Accessed on Sep 2, 2023.\n\n\n\nS. F. Gibson and F. Lanni. Diffraction by a Circular Aperture as a Model for Three-Dimensional Optical Microscopy. Journal of the Optical Society of America A 6, 1357–1367 (1989). Accessed on Jul 9, 2023.\n\n\n\nS. F. Gibson and F. Lanni. Experimental Test of an Analytical Model of Aberration in an Oil-Immersion Objective Lens Used in            Three-Dimensional Light Microscopy. Journal of the Optical Society of America A 8, 1601–1613 (1991). Accessed on Jul 8, 2023.\n\n\n\nJ. W. Goodman. Introduction to Fourier Optics. 3rd ed Edition (Roberts & Co, Englewood, Colo, 2005).\n\n\n\nB. M. Hanser, M. G. Gustafsson, D. A. Agard and J. W. Sedat. Phase Retrieval for High-Numerical-Aperture Optical Systems. Optics Letters 28, 801–803 (2003). Accessed on Jul 8, 2023.\n\n\n\nB. M. Hanser, M. G. Gustafsson, D. A. Agard and J. W. Sedat. *Phase-Retrieved Pupil Functions in Wide-Field Fluorescence Microscopy *. Journal of Microscopy 216, 32–48 (2004). Accessed on Jul 8, 2023.\n\n\n\nE. Hecht. Optics. 5 ed/fifth edition, global edition Edition (Pearson, Boston Columbus Indianapolis New York San Francisco Amsterdam Cape Town Dubai London Madrid Milan Munich, 2017).\n\n\n\nH. Hopkins and M. J. Yzuel. The Computation of Diffraction Patterns in the Presence of Aberrations. Optica Acta: International Journal of Optics 17, 157–182 (1970). Accessed on Jul 12, 2023.\n\n\n\nH. Kirshner, F. Aguet, D. Sage and M. Unser. 3-D PSF Fitting for Fluorescence Microscopy: Implementation and Localization Application. Journal of Microscopy 249, 13–25 (2013). Accessed on Jul 8, 2023.\n\n\n\nM. Leutenegger, R. Rao, R. A. Leitgeb and T. Lasser. Fast Focus Field Calculations. Optics Express 14, 11277–11291 (2006). Accessed on Jul 12, 2023.\n\n\n\nJ. Li, F. Xue and T. Blu. Fast and Accurate Three-Dimensional Point Spread Function Computation for Fluorescence Microscopy. Journal of the Optical Society of America A 34, 1029–1034 (2017). Accessed on Jul 8, 2023.\n\n\n\nJ. Li, F. Xue and T. Blu. Accurate 3D PSF Estimation from a Wide-Field Microscopy Image. In: 2018 IEEE 15th International Symposium on Biomedical Imaging (ISBI 2018) (Apr 2018); pp. 501–504.\n\n\n\nJ. Li, F. Xue, F. Qu, Y.-P. Ho and T. Blu. On-the-Fly Estimation of a Microscopy Point Spread Function. Optics Express 26, 26120 (2018).\n\n\n\nV. N. Mahajan. Optical Imaging and Aberrations: Wave Diffraction Optics (SPIE, 2011).\n\n\n\nA. Mikš and J. Novák. Point Spread Function of an Optical System with Defocus and Spherical Aberration - Analytical Formulas. Applied Optics 58, 5823–5829 (2019). Accessed on Jul 8, 2023.\n\n\n\nB. Richards, E. Wolf and D. Gabor. Electromagnetic Diffraction in Optical Systems, II. Structure of the Image Field in an Aplanatic            System. Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. Series A. Mathematical and Physical Sciences 253, 358–379 (1997). Accessed on Jul 12, 2023.\n\n\n\nC. S. Williams and O. A. Becklund. Introduction to the Optical Transfer Function (SPIE Press, 2002).\n\n\n\nE. Wolf and Y. Li. Conditions for the Validity of the Debye Integral Representation of Focused Fields. Optics Communications 39, 205–210 (1981). Accessed on Jul 12, 2023.\n\n\n\nE. Wolf and D. Gabor. Electromagnetic Diffraction in Optical Systems - I. An Integral Representation of the Image Field. Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. Series A. Mathematical and Physical Sciences 253, 349–357 (1997). Accessed on Jul 12, 2023.\n\n\n\nE. W. Weisstein. Apodization Function, https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ApodizationFunction.html. Accessed on Oct 5, 2023.\n\n\n\nE. W. Weisstein. Full Width at Half Maximum, https://mathworld.wolfram.com/FullWidthatHalfMaximum.html. Accessed on Oct 5, 2023.\n\n\n\nH. Yoo, I. Song and D.-G. Gweon. Measurement and Restoration of the Point Spread Function of Fluorescence Confocal Microscopy. Journal of Microscopy 221, 172–176 (2006). Accessed on Jul 8, 2023.\n\n\n\n","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"CurrentModule = TransferFunctions","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"There are two main categories of transfer function types that are provided by this package","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"Subtypes of TransferFunction which define a general transfer function model/measurement of a microscope   apparatus and \nSubtypes of SampledTransferFunction that may are created by providing a sampling scheme (most commonly   a pixel size) to the TransferFunction. These are used when you are working with specific acquisition with   known sampling rates/schemes.","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/#Transfer-Functions","page":"General Interface","title":"Transfer Functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"You can describe a transfer of a microscope with either the optical transfer function (OTF) the point spread     function (PSF) or the generalized pupil function. These descriptions are mostly interchangeable/convertible between each other.","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"note: Note\nIt may the case that a model can be in a closed form expression for some of these and must be approximated for the others, which may be a reason for the choice between them for a specific model.","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"You can get a transfer function of your optical setup by supplying parameters of the apparatus to a model transfer     function that is developed from the underlining physics of a microscope, then you will use a subtype of","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"ModelTransferFunction","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/#TransferFunctions.ModelTransferFunction","page":"General Interface","title":"TransferFunctions.ModelTransferFunction","text":"Union type for transfer function that is based on a physical model of an optical system. Contrary to a MeasuredTransferFunction, a ModelTransferFunction must be quantifiable at any point (in either spatial or frequency domain).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"Otherwise you can estimate the transfer function, most commonly by the means of an acquisition where the imaged sample is known such as sub-diffraction sized microspheres of known sizes. This approach will lead to a MeasuredTransferFunction","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"MeasuredTransferFunction","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/#TransferFunctions.MeasuredTransferFunction","page":"General Interface","title":"TransferFunctions.MeasuredTransferFunction","text":"An abstract type for the measurement of the transfer function of an optical system. It can be either a PSF measurement  MeasuredPSF or a MeasuredOTF.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/#Sampled-Transfer-Functions","page":"General Interface","title":"Sampled Transfer Functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/02_interface/","page":"General Interface","title":"General Interface","text":"<!– TODO:  <18-11-24> –>","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"CurrentModule = TransferFunctions","category":"page"},{"location":"#TransferFunctions.jl","page":"Home","title":"TransferFunctions.jl","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Transfer functions for optical systems","category":"page"},{"location":"#Package-Features","page":"Home","title":"Package Features","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"note: Note\nIf you want to know more about the model of image formation in optical systems (cameras, microscopes, etc.) see theory section.","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Types for OTF, PSF, MTF or PTF from a model or measured data\nConvert between OTF and PSF for models and measurements\nModels of transfer functions (OTF and PSF)\nDefine a transfer function from its Zernicke coefficients <!– TODO:  –>\nEstimate the Zernicke coefficients for a measured transfer function <!– TODO: –>","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/01_theory/#Theory-Overview","page":"Theory","title":"Theory Overview","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"pages/01_theory/","page":"Theory","title":"Theory","text":"note: Note\nAll of the models assume incoherent illumination sources (i.e. where the radiation from the source has inocoherent frequency light). This is an approximation for most optical setups, but one that is used almost universally (e.g. in fluorescence microscopy).","category":"page"},{"location":"pages/01_theory/","page":"Theory","title":"Theory","text":"<!– TODO: –>","category":"page"}]
